# {{ package_name }} — a pointer-only VE package

This package is a **workspace member with a pointer-only docs tree**: it carries
`external.yaml` interest edges naming documentation it consumes, and no
`docs/trunk/`. That is deliberate. A `docs/trunk/` would make this directory a new
*addressing root*, and a repository full of addressing roots is how references start
resolving to real-but-wrong documents.

This package owns no intent of its own. It has local names for the intent it reads.

## Where the governing docs live

{% if governing_tree_rel -%}
`{{ governing_tree_rel }}` — the nearest enclosing VE tree, the closest directory
above this one holding a `docs/trunk/`. Read these first:

- `{{ governing_tree_rel }}/docs/trunk/GOAL.md`
- `{{ governing_tree_rel }}/docs/trunk/SPEC.md`
- `{{ governing_tree_rel }}/docs/trunk/TESTING_PHILOSOPHY.md`

A bare reference (`docs/chunks/...`, `docs/subsystems/...`) in a file under this
package resolves against that tree — the nearest enclosing tree of *the file*, never
your current working directory and never the repository root. `ve` commands run here
resolve the same way, and say so on stderr when they redirect.
{% else -%}
**No VE tree governs this package.** Nothing above it holds a `docs/trunk/`{% if workspace_root_rel %} before
the workspace root (`{{ workspace_root_rel }}`){% endif %}, so a bare reference
(`docs/chunks/...`, `docs/subsystems/...`) written in a file here resolves to nothing
at all.

Every documentation reference from this package must therefore be explicit. Qualify
it with the tree that owns it:

    # Subsystem: {{ example_ref }}

Or record it as an interest edge below, so this package has a local name for it.
{% endif %}
## Intent this package consumes

{% if interests -%}
| Local pointer | Owned by | Why |
|---------------|----------|-----|
