Each row is an `external.yaml` pointing at another tree in this same working copy.
Nothing is copied and nothing is fetched: resolution is a manifest lookup and a file
read, so a target is always at the same commit as this package. Read one with
`ve external show <artifact>`.
{% else -%}
Nothing yet.
{% endif %}
Record a new dependency from the package whose code depends on it:

```bash
ve external point <member> docs/<type>/<name> --why "what this package depends on"
```

Point when readers multiply; promote when writers change. A pointer says "this
package reads that intent" and leaves ownership with the tree whose code enforces it.
{% if members %}
Trees you can point at: {% for member in members %}`{{ member }}`{{ ", " if not loop.last }}{% endfor %}.
{% endif %}
## Working in this package

- Artifacts belong to the tree that owns the intent. Do not run `ve chunk create`
  here expecting a local chunk — there is no local chunk namespace, which is the
  point. Create the chunk in the tree whose code the work changes.
- Add code backreferences as usual, but qualify anything that crosses the boundary:
  `# Subsystem: <member>::docs/subsystems/<name>` for intent owned elsewhere.
- `ve workspace validate`, run from the workspace root, checks that every reference
  in this package still resolves.

## Opting into a full tree later

Do this when this package starts *owning* intent — when its own code enforces an
invariant that needs somewhere to be written down:

```bash
ve init --project-dir .        # creates docs/trunk/ and the artifact directories
ve workspace add {{ package_name }} <path-from-workspace-root>    # if not already registered
```

Know the cost first: a `docs/trunk/` makes this directory an **addressing root**.
Bare references in files beneath it stop resolving upward{% if governing_tree_rel %} to `{{ governing_tree_rel }}`{% endif %} and
start resolving here, so every bare reference already written in this package changes
meaning. Promote intent deliberately; point at it by default.
